# Or could property rights fix it?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does the Coase theorem say about bargaining over harm? Why do transaction costs usually spoil the bargain?"
next: {"title":"Tax or quota?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/45-tax-or-quota.cast"}

## The doctor and the confectioner
sources:
    - id: sturges1879
      title: Sturges v Bridgman (1879) 11 Ch D 852
      authors: Court of Appeal (England)
      year: 1879
      finding: A Wigmore Street confectioner's pestles and mortars disturbed a Wimpole Street doctor's new consulting room; the court granted the doctor an injunction.
    - id: coase1960
      title: The Problem of Social Cost
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1960
      finding: 'Harm is reciprocal; with costless bargaining the outcome does not depend on the legal assignment of rights. Discusses Sturges v Bridgman. Journal of Law and Economics 3: 1–44.'

    shape kitchen shape rect x 370 y 395 width 260 height 290 hidden true
    polygon kroof points [[225,540],[370,615],[500,540]] hidden true
    shape room shape rect x 630 y 395 width 260 height 290 hidden true
    polygon rroof points [[500,540],[630,615],[775,540]] hidden true
    icon mortar size 110 x 370 y 440 or ["mortar","bowl"] icon_look picture of "mortar and pestle" hidden true
    text lab_conf "Confectioner" #b5482e x 370 y 355 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon steth size 110 x 630 y 440 or ["doctor"] icon_look picture of stethoscope hidden true
    text lab_doc "Doctor" #2f6b8f x 630 y 355 font_size 28 hidden true
    path noise #b5482e points [[455,470],[475,490],[495,460],[515,495],[535,465],[555,490]] hidden true
    text city "London, 1879" #8f887c x 115 y 600 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon gavel size 80 x 885 y 540 or ["judge","scales"] icon_look picture of gavel hidden true
    text court "Court:\ndoctor wins" #2f6b8f cites ["sturges1879"] x 885 y 450 hidden true
    text val_k "Mortars: $200/week" #b5482e x 370 y 200 font_size 30 hidden true
    text val_d "Quiet: $100/week" #2f6b8f x 630 y 200 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow pay #3d3833 from.x 400 from.y 295 to.x 600 to.y 295 hidden true
    text pay_lab "$150" x 440 y 320 font_size 30 hidden true
    text goes_on "Pounding\ngoes on" #b5482e x 885 y 450 hidden true
    portrait coase x 880 y 470 width 170 of "Ronald Coase" hidden true
    arrow harm1 #b5482e from.x 400 from.y 310 to.x 600 to.y 310 hidden true
    arrow harm2 #2f6b8f from.x 600 from.y 280 to.x 400 to.y 280 hidden true
    text both "Harm runs\nboth ways" #3d3833 x 885 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The doctor and the confectioner"

Could pollution be fixed without a tax, just by letting the people involved strike a deal? Start with a noisy  (@draw city@)kitchen in London, in 1879.
    draw kitchen kroof

B: A confectioner on Wigmore Street pounds sugar with heavy mortars, as he has done for years.
    draw mortar lab_conf

Then the doctor next door builds a consulting room right against the kitchen wall, and throug (@draw noise@)h the pounding he can't hear his patients' chests.
    draw room rroof steth lab_doc

B: Most of us would say: he makes the noise, so he should stop. And the  (@draw gavel court@)court agreed. It ordered the pounding to stop.
    highlight noise

    pause 0.3

Now give it numbers, illustrative ones. Say the mortars earn the confec (@draw val_k@)tioner two hundred dollars a week, and a quiet room is worth on (@draw val_d@)e hundred to the doctor.
    highlight mortar steth

B: So the doctor holds the right to quiet. Does the pounding stop?
    highlight court

    quiz "Your guess: does the pounding stop?" judge false voice b
        right: Here is what a deal can do.
        * Yes: the court ordered it
        * No: the confectioner buys the right
        * Only if the doctor pays

Not necessarily. The confectioner can offer the  (@draw pay pay_lab@)doctor, say, a hundred and fifty a week to put up with it, or to move his consulting room.
    erase court gavel

B: And the doctor takes it, because he's fifty dollars b (@highlight val_d@)etter off than with his quiet room.
    draw goes_on

That was Ronald Coase's point in 1960, and he used this very case. Stoppin (@erase pay pay_lab goes_on@)g the confectioner harms him, just as his noise h (@draw harm1@)arms the doctor. The harm (@draw harm2@) runs both ways.
    draw coase

B: So the question isn (@draw both@)'t who's to blame. It's which use is wo (@highlight val_k val_d effect underline@)rth more, and a deal can find that out.
    erase coase

## Whoever owns the bay
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Paint waste (tonnes)
    y_label: $ per tonne
    demand:
      label: Yard's gain
    supply:
      label: Fishers' harm
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      label: ''
      p_label: ''
      q_label: Q*
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 760
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: coase1960
      title: The Problem of Social Cost
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1960
      finding: 'With costless bargaining, the efficient outcome is reached whichever party holds the legal right; the right decides only who pays. Journal of Law and Economics 3: 1–44.'
    - id: stigler1966
      title: The Theory of Price (3rd ed.)
      authors: George J. Stigler
      year: 1966
      finding: Stigler stated Coase's result and named it the Coase theorem.

    icon boat size 90 x 900 y 580 or ["boat","ship"] icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    icon fish size 90 x 900 y 430 icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    arrow arr1 #2f6b8f from.data [2,8] to.data [47,8] hidden true
    text t1 "Fishers own:\nyard pays" #2f6b8f at.data [17,37] font_size 26 hidden true
    arrow arr2 #b5482e from.data [95,8] to.data [53,8] hidden true

    card title "Whoever owns the bay"

B: In that kitchen, a deal decided what happened, not the court. Does that work for pollution? Take H (@draw boat@)arbourtown's boatyard, which washes its paint waste into the bay.
    draw axes

B: This curve is what each extra tonne of dumping saves the boatyard. The first  (@point at.data [8,92] gesture tap@)tonnes save it a lot, the last o (@point at.data [92,8] gesture tap@)nes almost nothing.
    draw demand_curve label_D

And the fishers pay for it with a smaller catch?
    draw fish

B: Yes, and each extra tonne harms them more than the one before. That's the rising curve.
    draw supply_curve label_S

Say the fishers own the bay. No dumping without their consent.
    draw t1

B: Then the boatyard buys the right, tonne by tonne, as long a (@draw arr1@)s a tonne saves it more than it harms the fishers. It stops here, w (@draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Qstar@)here the curves cross.
    highlight t1

And if the boatyard owns the bay instead?
    text t2 "Yard owns:\nfishers pay" #b5482e at.data [83,50] font_size 26

B: Then the fishers pay it to dump less, cutting from the top, as long a (@draw arr2@)s each cut spares them more than it costs the yard. They stop at the v (@highlight equilibrium_point@)ery same point.
    highlight t2

The same amount of waste either way. Only  (@highlight t1 t2@)the direction of the money changes.
    highlight label_Qstar effect circle

George Stigler named this the Coase theorem: if bargaining costs nothing, the deal lands (@highlight equilibrium_point effect pulse@) on the efficient amount, whoever holds the right. The right only decides who pays whom.
    text theorem "Coase\ntheorem" cites ["coase1960","stigler1966"] x 900 y 280 font_size 30

    erase arr1 arr2 t1 t2

B: Try it: drag the fishers' harm curve up, and watch where the deal lands.
    explore

## The bargain that never happens
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Paint waste (tonnes)
    y_label: $ per tonne
    demand:
      label: Yard's gain
    supply:
      label: Fishers' harm
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      label: ''
      p_label: ''
      q_label: Q*
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 760
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: coase1988
      title: The Firm, the Market, and the Law
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1988
      finding: Coase describes the world of zero transaction costs as the world of modern economic theory, which he hoped to persuade economists to leave; with positive transaction costs the legal assignment of rights matters.
    - id: kkt1990
      title: Experimental Tests of the Endowment Effect and the Coase Theorem
      authors: Daniel Kahneman, Jack L. Knetsch, Richard H. Thaler
      year: 1990
      finding: 'Owners of a mug asked roughly twice what non-owners would pay; far fewer mugs traded than the Coase theorem predicts. Journal of Political Economy 98(6): 1325–1348.'

    icon f0 size 56 x 870 y 600 or ["people","person"] icon_look picture of family hidden true
    icon f1 size 56 x 932 y 600 or ["people","person"] icon_look picture of family hidden true
    icon f2 size 56 x 870 y 534 or ["people","person"] icon_look picture of family hidden true
    icon f3 size 56 x 932 y 534 or ["people","person"] icon_look picture of family hidden true
    icon f4 size 56 x 839 y 468 or ["people","person"] icon_look picture of family hidden true
    text fam_t "200\nfamilies" #2f6b8f x 900 y 385 font_size 28 hidden true
    text tc "Transaction\ncosts" cites ["coase1988"] x 900 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    dot nodeal #b5482e at.data [95,0] hidden true
    region lost x_from 50 x_to 95 between ["supply_curve","demand_curve"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text lost_t "Lost" #b5482e at.data [80,50] font_size 30 hidden true
    text endow "Endowment effect:\nowners ask ~2×\nwhat buyers offer" cites ["kkt1990"] x 900 y 380 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The bargain that never happens"

B: A neat result. But Harbourtown has two hundr (@draw f0 f1 f2 f3 f4 parallel true@)ed fishing families, (@draw fam_t@) not one doctor.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Qstar

Right. Someone has to find them all, agree what the harm is worth, and collect the money.
    highlight f0 f1 f2 f3 f4

B: And any one family can hold out for more, or stay quiet and let the others pay.
    fade f1 f3 to 0.25

Those are transacti (@draw tc@)on costs: the cost of striking and enforcing the deal itself. When they ea (@highlight tc@)t up the gain, no deal happens.
    erase f0 f1 f2 f3 f4 fam_t

B: So the waste stays wherever the law left it. If the yard owns the bay, it dumps  (@draw nodeal@)all the way to here.
    fade equilibrium_point guide_lines label_Qstar to 0.3

And this triangle is lost: harm to  (@draw lost_t@)the fishers bigger than the yard's savings, an (@highlight lost@)d nobody can afford to buy it away.
    draw lost

B: Then who holds the right matters after all.
    point at.ref nodeal gesture circle

Which was Coase's own point. He said the world without transaction costs was the world of economic theory, the one he hoped economists would leave.
    highlight tc effect underline

B: And a second crack: the endowment effect. Owners a (@draw endow@)sk about twice what buyers offer, so whoever starts with the right tends to keep it, even when a deal costs nothing.

Either way, the  (@highlight label_Qstar@)law's first move sha (@highlight nodeal@)pes the outcome.
    erase endow

## How far does a deal reach?
sources:
    - id: hs1982
      title: 'The Coase Theorem: Some Experimental Tests'
      authors: Elizabeth Hoffman, Matthew L. Spitzer
      year: 1982
      finding: 'In two- and three-person bargaining experiments with known payoffs, about 89.5% of 114 decisions reached the efficient (joint-payoff-maximising) outcome. Journal of Law and Economics 25(1): 73–98.'
    - id: cheung1973
      title: 'The Fable of the Bees: An Economic Investigation'
      authors: Steven N. S. Cheung
      year: 1973
      finding: 'Washington State beekeepers and orchard owners contracted for pollination services and honey, pricing the spillover privately. Journal of Law and Economics 16(1): 11–33.'
    - id: farnsworth1999
      title: Do Parties to Nuisance Cases Bargain After Judgment? A Glimpse Inside the Cathedral
      authors: Ward Farnsworth
      year: 1999
      finding: 'In twenty nuisance cases there was no bargaining after judgment in any; lawyers cited animosity between the parties among the reasons. University of Chicago Law Review 66: 373.'

    arrow line #3d3833 from.x 90 from.y 330 to.x 910 to.y 330 hidden true
    text lo "2" #8f887c x 100 y 292 font_size 34 hidden true
    text hi "Millions" #8f887c x 850 y 292 font_size 34 hidden true
    text axis_t "People who must agree" #8f887c x 500 y 292 font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_lab "Lab groups" #2f6b8f cites ["hs1982"] x 170 y 385 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_bees "Bees & orchards" #2f6b8f cites ["cheung1973"] x 250 y 440 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_court "Nuisance cases:\nfail even at 2" #b5482e cites ["farnsworth1999"] x 230 y 220 font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_smog "City smog" #b5482e x 700 y 385 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_clim "Climate" #b5482e x 850 y 440 font_size 36 hidden true
    text works "Deals work" #5f8a4f x 190 y 505 font_size 42 hidden true
    text fails "Deals fail" #b5482e x 810 y 505 font_size 42 hidden true
    text conf1 "Zero costs → efficient: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 625 font_size 32 hidden true
    text conf2 "Few parties: often works — mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 580 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon tree size 150 x 330 y 430 or ["tree"] icon_look picture of "deciduous tree" hidden true
    icon view size 150 x 670 y 430 or ["mountain","sunrise"] icon_look picture of "sunrise over mountains" hidden true
    text ann "Ann: $300" #b5482e x 330 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ben "Ben: $1,000" #2f6b8f x 670 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mth work ["Gain = $200 − $100 = $100","Price: between $100 and $200","Deal if gain > transaction cost"] x 500 y 420 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "How far does a deal reach?"

Transaction costs decide whether a deal can stand  (@draw axis_t@)in for the law. So how often do rea (@highlight lo hi@)l deals happen?
    draw line lo hi

B: In the lab, often. In Hoffman and Spitzer's 1982 experiments, small groups  (@draw works@)reached the efficient deal in about nine  (@highlight c_lab@)decisions out of ten.
    draw c_lab

And outside the lab?
    highlight works

B: Bees. In 1973 Steven Cheung found apple growers and beekeepers in Washington State writing contracts for pollination (@highlight c_bees@), a spillover priced by private deals.
    draw c_bees

But neighbours who end up in court? Ward Farnsworth followed twe (@draw c_court@)nty nuisance cases, and in not one did the parties bargain after (@highlight c_court effect underline@) the judgment.
    highlight lo

B: The lawyers said bitterness was a big reason. And smog over a city, or the (@draw c_clim@) climate, involves millions. No (@draw fails@) table is that big.
    draw c_smog

So: with no transaction costs the deal is efficient, which is logic, so consensus. That a (@draw conf2@) few close parties often strike such deals is mostly agreed.
    draw conf1

    hide line lo hi axis_t c_lab c_bees c_court c_smog c_clim works fails conf1 conf2

    draw mathq

    ask "The arithmetic of the kitchen deal: open the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

In the kitchen, the deal creates two hundred minus one hundred: a hundred dollars a week to share.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

B: Any payment between one and two hundred leaves both better off. A hundred and fifty splits the gain evenly.
    draw mth_line_2

And the deal happens only if that hundred is bigger than the cost of striking it.
    draw mth_line_3

    erase mth

@test

    erase mathq

    hide line lo hi axis_t c_lab c_bees c_court c_smog c_clim works fails conf1 conf2

    cards sortc check each
        Deal likely: Hedge between two gardens
        Deal unlikely: City-wide traffic fumes
        * "Bar music, one flat above" bin "Deal likely"
        Deal unlikely: Plastic in the ocean

    ask "Could a private deal fix it? Sort each case." on sortc store f stage own after clear voice b
        right: Hedge and bar music: deal likely; city fumes and ocean plastic: deal unlikely. A deal works only where a few people can meet at one table.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. A few people, deal likely: the hedge, the bar's music. Millions, deal unlikely: city fumes, ocean plastic.

    draw tree ann view ben parallel true

    quiz "Ann's tree blocks Ben's view. The law lets Ann keep it. The tree is worth $300 to Ann, the view $1,000 to Ben, and they talk easily. What happens?"
        right: Ben pays Ann somewhere between $300 and $1,000, and the tree comes down. The view is worth more.
        wrong: Compare what each one values, and ask who could buy out whom: here, Ben pays Ann.
        * The tree stays: the law protects it
        + Ben pays Ann to cut it down
        * Ann pays Ben to accept it

    quiz "Now the law gives Ben the right to his view instead. What changes?" voice b
        right: The tree still goes: Ann won't pay $1,000 to keep a $300 tree. Only who pays changes.
        wrong: Would Ann pay $1,000 to keep a tree worth $300 to her? No: the tree still goes.
        + The tree still goes, but Ann gets nothing for it
        * The tree now stays
        * Ben must pay twice as much

    erase tree ann view ben

    show line lo hi axis_t c_lab c_bees c_court c_smog c_clim works fails conf1 conf2

So could property rights fix pollution? Where a few people can sit at one table, yes, and the law only decides who pays. Where millions breathe the same air, the bargain never happens, and we're back to taxes and rules.
    highlight works fails

